<LessonShell chapterNumber={3} lessonNumber={4}>

## The Answers Karel Threw Away

`front_is_clear()` handed something back every time you called it. So did `beepers_present()`, and every other sensor. You fed those answers straight into a `while` or an `if` and never once kept one.

You could not have kept one. There was nowhere to put it and no way to look at it.

Now there is. Those answers were values, of a type, and this lesson is about that type — the last of the four you need, and the one that unlocks everything Chapter 2 kept promising.

## `True` and `False`

The type is called `bool`, short for **boolean**, after George Boole, who worked out the algebra of true and false a century before there were computers to run it on.

It has exactly two values: `True` and `False`. Both capitalised — `true` will get you a `NameError`.

<PythonEnvironment config={boolValueConfig} />

Look at the panel. `ready` holds `True` the same way a name holds `7` or `"Karel"`. It is not a special kind of name or a magic marker; it is an ordinary variable holding an ordinary value, and everything you learned in Lesson 3.1 applies to it unchanged.

That is the whole idea of this lesson, and everything else follows from it: **a condition is an expression, and expressions have values.**

## The Six Comparison Operators

Karel's sensors were written for you. In Python you build your own, out of six operators that take two values and hand back a boolean:

| Operator | Asks                       |
| -------- | -------------------------- |
| `==`     | are these equal?           |
| `!=`     | are these different?       |
| `<`      | is the left one smaller?   |
| `>`      | is the left one bigger?    |
| `<=`     | smaller than or equal to?  |
| `>=`     | bigger than or equal to?   |

<PythonEnvironment config={compareConfig} />

Every line printed `True` or `False`. Nothing was compared *for* anything — no `if` in sight. The comparison happened, produced a value, and `print` showed it to you. That is worth sitting with, because it is the thing beginners most often fail to see: `age >= 18` is not a special piece of `if` grammar. It is an expression, like `2 + 2`, and its answer happens to be a boolean.

### `==` Is Not `=`

Lesson 3.1 promised this one would arrive here.

- `=` **assigns**. It is an instruction: work out the right side, put the name on the answer.
- `==` **asks**. It is a question: are these two things equal? It changes nothing.

```python
fuel = 50    # fuel now holds 50
fuel == 50   # True - and fuel is untouched
```

Typing one where you meant the other is the most common beginner bug in every language that has both. In Python you usually get away with it loudly: `if fuel = 50:` is a `SyntaxError`, so the program refuses to start rather than misbehaving quietly. That is a kindness, and not every language offers it.

### Three Comparisons That Surprise People

<PythonEnvironment config={gotchaConfig} />

**`"Yes" == "yes"` is `False`.** String comparison is exact, and capital letters are different characters from small ones. Any program that asks a person a yes-or-no question has to deal with this. Chapter 5 gives you the tidy fix; until then, either state the exact wording you expect or check for more than one spelling with `or`.

**`0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is `False`.** Lesson 3.2 warned you this was coming, and now you have the operator that proves it. The sum really is `0.30000000000000004`, and `==` is exact, so it says no. **Never compare floats with `==`.** Compare integers, or compare with a tolerance — round both sides first if you must.

**`"10" == 10` is `False`, and it is not an error.** A string is not a number, so they are not equal, and Python says so calmly rather than complaining. This is the one that bites hardest with `input()`, because `input()` always hands back text: `if answer == 10:` will be `False` for ever, no matter what the person types. Convert first — `int(answer) == 10` — and Lesson 3.6 goes through the conversions properly.

> `<` and `>` work on strings too, comparing alphabetically: `"apple" < "banana"` is `True`. The catch is that **every capital letter sorts before every small one**, so `"Zebra" < "apple"` is also `True`. Useful once you know it, baffling until you do.

## `and`, `or`, `not` — and Why `not` Finally Matters

You met all three in Lesson 1.6, combining Karel's sensors. They work exactly as they did:

- `and` is `True` only when **both** sides are true
- `or` is `True` when **either** side is true
- `not` flips whatever follows it

What changes is `not`. Lesson 1.6 told you that you would hardly ever need it, because every Karel sensor came with a ready-made opposite — `front_is_blocked()` already meant `not front_is_clear()`. And it closed with a promise:

> Those twins are a courtesy of Karel's world, not a feature of Python. In the next chapter you will start asking questions that nobody wrote an opposite for, and `not` is how you flip _any_ condition at all.

This is that chapter. Nobody has written an opposite for `len(name) > 3`, or for `name == "Ada"`. You write `not` in front of it, and now you have one.

```python
if not name == "Ada":
  print("No record of that name")
```

Though where an operator has a natural opposite, prefer it — `name != "Ada"` reads better than `not name == "Ada"`. Save `not` for the conditions that have no twin.

## Conditions Are Values, So Name Them

Here is the payoff for the whole lesson. If a condition is an expression with a value, a variable can hold it:

<PythonEnvironment config={storedConfig} />

`old_enough = age >= 18` works out the comparison and stores the answer. The `if` then reads `if old_enough:` — which says what it means in English.

This is not a trick. It is ordinary good style, and it earns its keep the moment a condition gets long enough to be hard to read, or gets used twice. Exercise 3 does exactly this.

Notice too that `if old_enough:` has no `== True` on the end. It does not need one — `old_enough` is already a boolean, and `if` wants a boolean. Writing `if old_enough == True:` is not wrong, just redundant, in the way that "is it true that it is true?" is redundant.

## `if`, `elif`, `else` Come Home

You have written all of this before, in Lesson 1.6. Nothing about the syntax has changed:

```python
if fuel >= 80:
  print("GO")
elif fuel >= 40:
  print("CAUTION")
else:
  print("ABORT")
```

The colon, the indented block, `elif` for the next question, `else` for everything left over — all as it was with Karel.

What has changed is the **conditions**. With Karel you had a fixed menu of about a dozen questions, all of them about walls, beepers or compass direction. Now you can ask about anything you can compute: a number a person typed, the length of a word, whether two names match, whether a total has passed a limit.

Two reminders from 1.6, both of which still bite:

- **Only the first matching branch runs.** In the example above, a fuel level of `90` prints `GO` and never even asks the `elif`. Once a branch matches, the rest of the chain is skipped entirely.
- **Order matters, and overlapping bands are where it shows.** If you put `elif fuel >= 40:` first, then `90` would match it and print `CAUTION`, and the `fuel >= 80` branch could never run for any value at all. When your bands overlap, go from the most demanding to the least.

